## Directions panel (`MapMarkerDirectionsPanel`)

The dashed bird-eye polyline is paired with a shared "Directions" sheet that lets the user choose the route's start and end (with a swap button) before committing to first-person navigation. **The same panel is reused for POI destinations (Foyer / Entrance / Arena) and seat destinations** — the panel branches on `preview.kind` (`'poi'` vs `'seat'`).

### Lifecycle

```
User picks destination
  ├─ POI:  click map-marker → `openMapMarkerDirectionsPreview`
  └─ Seat: type seat in `SeatNavigateWidget` (bird-eye only) → `openSeatDirectionsPreview`
        ↓ (sets `mapMarkerDirectionsPreview` in usePoiNavigation)
Panel mounts, dashed bird-eye route drawn, bubbles at endpoints
        ↓ user presses swap (optional) → swaps inputs, redraws polyline
        ↓ user presses "Start navigation"
  ├─ POI branch:  adoptPoiRouteTarget(routeKey, { primPath, endPos, displayLabel })
  │              + `mapMarkerDirectionsStart` → Kit
  └─ Seat branch: setActiveSeatRouteId(seatKey)
                  + setSeatRouteSwapState({ fromSeat: swapped, displayLabel })
                  + `seatDirectionsStart` → Kit
        ↓ Kit fades out, switches to first-person, computes route, sends `seatDirectionsResolved`
Panel dismissed, in-flight navigation pill shown
```

### Two paired state items per kind (must always move together)

To prevent silent label/UX drift, each kind has a **single combined setter**. Never touch the underlying ref/state directly from outside the hook — go through the API so both move at once:

| Kind | Combined setter | Underlying fields | What they drive |
|---|---|---|---|
| POI | `adoptPoiRouteTarget(routeKey, { ..., displayLabel })` | `poiRouteDisplayLabel` (state) + `lastPoiDisplayNameRef` (ref) | In-flight pill + arrival overlay text |
| Seat | `setSeatRouteSwapState({ fromSeat, displayLabel })` / `clearSeatRouteSwapState()` | `seatRouteFromSeatRef` (ref) + `seatRouteDisplayLabel` (state) | Swap-arrival suppression + in-flight pill override |

The seat ref is intentionally a `ref` (not state) because `seatHandlers.ts` (`seatTeleportResult`) and `navigationHandlers.ts` (arrival paths) need to read it synchronously at event-dispatch time without re-render delays. The POI display name is also a ref for the same reason — arrival happens synchronously in handlers.

### Navmesh-POI variant (restroom / quiet zone)

When a list-card item from `RestroomWidget` or `QuietZoneWidget` is opened on the bird-eye stream, the panel shows the same POI shape as Foyer / Arena but the destination is a **navmesh-resolved prim path**, not a `PlayerSpawnPoint_*`. Two extra fields thread through to make this work:

| Field | Where | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `useNavmeshPoiTeleport: true` | `MapMarkerSheetData` (web) → `MapMarkerDirectionsPreview.poi` | The destination has no authored spawn point; resolve `start_pose` from the prim's xform instead. Also unlocks the swap arrow on the panel for these POIs. |
| `endpointPath` | `mapMarkerDirectionsStart` payload (always set for navmesh POIs) | USD prim path of the POI (e.g. `/World/restroom_03`); Kit calls `UsdGeom.Xformable.ComputeLocalToWorldTransform` to derive `end_pos`. |
| `startEndpointPath` | `mapMarkerDirectionsStart` payload (set on **swap**) | Same prim path used as the **start** when the user pressed swap; Kit derives `start_pos` from it the same way and builds `start_pose` for `_async_first_person_enter`. The "back to last FP" destination is already encoded by `endUseStoredFp`. |

The forward direction (current FP → POI) does not need `start_pose` — the player is already in FP, so the FP-enter path resolves the start from stored state. The swapped direction (POI → last FP) **does** need `start_pose` because we have to teleport into the POI's location and orient toward `lastFpPos` before the polyline appears.

Player-distance computation for the list itself (the per-item time/distance) uses **`/younite/player/lastFpPos*`** when bird-eye is the active camera — the bird-eye player position isn't on the navmesh, so falling back to the last known FP position keeps the prelist measurements meaningful. See `route-measurement.mdc` → "Bird-eye player ref" for the helper.

A flag pin is shown immediately on sheet open via the `birdEyePinShowAtPrim` event (Web → Kit), so the user sees the destination on the map before pressing either Get-directions or Teleport. The pin lifecycle uses the same `set_pin_marker` / `birdEyePinDismiss` pipeline as the ad-hoc click pin (see `bird-eye-pin-anywhere.mdc`).

**Reverse routes (POI → outdoor last-FP)** are now produced by the same symmetric `plan_route_to_position` as forward routes: the corridor router picks the best entrance to **exit** through (closest entrance to the FP destination by NavMesh distance), instead of looping out at whatever ring waypoint happens to be XZ-nearest to the FP. No payload change — this is automatic and transparent to the panel. See `waypoint-routing.mdc` → "Bidirectional endpoint selection".

### Swap mode behaviour

When the user swaps inputs the destination becomes the player's previously-stored FP position (carb settings `lastFpPos*`):

- **POI swap (Foyer / Arena)**: the panel passes `displayLabel = t('search.directionsCurrentPosition')` to `adoptPoiRouteTarget` so the in-flight pill and arrival overlay both read "Current position" instead of the POI name. Start endpoint is the POI's `spawnPoint`, sent via `startSpawnPoint` on `mapMarkerDirectionsStart`.
- **POI swap (navmesh POI: restroom / quiet zone)**: same display-label pattern, but the start endpoint is the POI's USD prim path sent via **`startEndpointPath`** (because there is no authored spawn point). Kit resolves the prim's xform to a world position and builds `start_pose` from it — see "Bird-eye → FP black-screen handoff" below for the body-yaw computation.
- **Seat swap**: the panel passes `setSeatRouteSwapState({ fromSeat: true, displayLabel: t('search.directionsCurrentPosition') })`. The `fromSeat` ref tells `handleAutoMoveStatus` and `handleRouteArrival` to fire `schedulePoiArrival` (POI-style overlay) instead of `scheduleSeatArrivalCelebration` — because the player is arriving at their previous location, not at the seat. The seat is the *origin* in this case.

### Strict-Mode pattern in arrival handlers

Both `handleAutoMoveStatus` and `handleRouteArrival` follow this strict pattern when reading the swap ref:

```ts
const fromSeat = s.seatRouteFromSeatRef.current;   // capture ONCE
if (reason === 'arrived') s.clearSeatRouteSwapState(); // clear OUTSIDE updater
s.setMovingToSeatId((prev) => {
    // ... use captured `fromSeat` (closure-stable) to branch celebration ...
});
```

Reading + clearing the ref **outside** the functional updater is required because React Strict Mode double-invokes updaters in dev — if we mutated the ref inside, the second pass would see `false` and fire the wrong celebration. Capturing into `fromSeat` once makes both invocations agree.

### Preflight check (seat only)

Bird-eye seat actions (both "Get directions" and "Teleport me there") run a synchronous RPC against Kit before triggering any view-transition, because:

- An invalid seat would otherwise leave the user stuck on a black "Switching to first person…" overlay forever (the executor returns silently → `viewTransitionReady` never fires).
- The directions panel would open and then fail when Kit can't resolve the end position.

The RPC is `seatTeleportFromBirdEyeCheck` → `seatTeleportFromBirdEyeCheckResult`, awaited via `requestSeatTeleportFromBirdEyeCheck()` in `seatBirdEyeTeleportRpc.ts`. Both buttons go through the same helper.

### Wheelchair labels in the directions panel

After the **`postprocess_wheelchair_wc_to_row6.py`** + regen pipeline, wheelchair seats use the same **section–row–seat** id shape as walking (e.g. `C-6-1`). `SeatNavigateWidget` passes that full id into `openSeatDirectionsPreview` via `t('seat.label', …)` — no special-case `WC`/`R` stripping in the widget. **`formatSeatLabel`** in `AppStream.tsx` still has a **legacy branch** for old bookmarks / stale keys shaped like `C-WC-R1` (row `WC`, seat `R`+digits): those display as "Section C, Seat 1" for continuity.

### Route ready gating (bird-eye → first-person directions, FP "Get directions", and POI/quiet-zone routes)

**The problem we're solving.** Before this gate existed, the moment the user pressed "Start navigation" (or "Get directions" inside FP) the panel snapped shut and the floating play FAB appeared even though Kit hadn't finished pathfinding yet. Two failure modes resulted:

1. **Bird-eye → FP directions:** the black "Switching to first person…" overlay faded back in *before* the polyline was drawn, then the route popped in late — sometimes never if the path query failed.
2. **FP "Get directions":** the FAB appeared instantly; clicking it before `lastSeatPositionRef` / `lastPoiEndPosRef` filled would silently no-op (auto-move had no `endPos` to send).

**Mechanism.** `useNavmeshCore` exposes `routeReadyByRouteId: Record<string, boolean>` plus two setters: `markRouteCalculating(routeId)` (writes `false`) and `markRouteReady(routeId, ready)`. Every initiator of a user-facing route calls `markRouteCalculating` synchronously *before* sending the Kit request:

| Initiator | Route id |
|---|---|
| `MapMarkerDirectionsPanel` "Start navigation" (POI / seat) | `poi_nav` / `seat_nav` |
| `useSeatNavigation.handleSeatNavigate` (FP seat panel "Get directions") | `seat_nav` |
| `usePoiNavigation.handlePoiClick` / `forceActivatePoiRoute` | `poi_nav` |
| `useQuietZoneNavigation.handleQuietZoneClick` | `quiet_zone_nav` |

Kit's `navmesh_route_extension._on_route_waypoints` relays a **`navmeshRouteReady { routeId }`** event to web whenever the underlying `navmeshRouteWaypoints` succeeds for one of `seat_nav | poi_nav | quiet_zone_nav | exit_nav`. `navigationHandlers.handleNavmeshRouteReady` calls `markRouteReady(routeId, true)`. Failure paths (`navmeshRouteError`, route arrival/teardown, `markRouteCalculating` on a fresh request) all flip the flag back to `false`.

**Where the gate is enforced.** `AppUIContext` derives the `*StreamOverlayActive` flags with an extra `nav.routeReadyByRouteId[routeId] === true` clause:

```ts
const seatStreamOverlayActive =
    !isViewer && stream.streamReady &&
    !!nav.activeSeatRouteId &&
    !nav.activeSeatRouteId.includes(':') &&            // exclude `unreachable:` etc
    nav.routeReadyByRouteId['seat_nav'] === true;       // ← the gate

const restroomStreamOverlayActive =
    !isViewer && stream.streamReady &&
    !!nav.activePoiRouteId &&
    !(nav.mapMarkerDirectionsPreview != null && !nav.movingToPoiId) &&
    nav.routeReadyByRouteId['poi_nav'] === true;        // ← the gate

const quietStreamOverlayActive =
    !isViewer && stream.streamReady &&
    nav.quietZoneWidgetOpen &&
    !!nav.activeQuietZoneRouteId &&
    nav.routeReadyByRouteId['quiet_zone_nav'] === true; // ← the gate
```

**Net UX wins:**

- The seat / restroom / quiet-zone panels **stay open** during the calculating window (their visibility is driven by widget-open state, not the overlay flag, so the panel naturally keeps showing while the FAB stays hidden).
- The play FAB and stream nav overlay only appear once a real polyline exists, so clicking it always works.
- `navmeshRouteError` keeps the gate closed — the panel inline-errors, no FAB ever flashes.

**Bird-eye → FP black-screen handoff.** This is the dynamic counterpart on the Kit side; both `MapMarkerDirectionsHandler._run` (in `feature_commands_service/map_marker_directions.py`) and `SeatDirectionsHandler._run_directions` (in `feature_commands_service/seat_directions.py`) use it:

1. They call `svc.async_first_person_enter(spawn_point, dispatch_ready=False)` — same LOD/navmesh prep as a normal FP teleport, **but `viewTransitionReady` is held back**. For POI swap / pin-anywhere / navmesh-POI (restroom / quiet zone) starts without a named spawn, the handler composes the route **first**, derives `body_yaw_deg` from the first polyline segment (`directions_utils.yaw_from_polyline`), then calls `TeleportService.teleport_to_coordinates(x, y, z, world_body_yaw_deg=yaw)` so the player lands at the route origin facing the dashed polyline. Navmesh POIs that have no `PlayerSpawnPoint_*` (restrooms / quiet zones) arrive as `startEndpointPath` on `mapMarkerDirectionsStart` and are resolved from the prim's xform before the teleport.
2. After `seatDirectionsResolved` / `mapMarkerDirectionsStart` triggers `navmeshRouteCalculate` (with `useComposer:true` for map-marker / pin / cross-island flows), they `await svc.await_navmesh_route_settled(route_id, timeout_sec=20.0)` which resolves on the next `navmeshRouteWaypoints` event for that id (or times out / errors). The route engine's single `_compute_path()` runs `RouteComposer.compose(...)` regardless — there is no `set_route_with_preset` shortcut anymore.
3. Only **then** do they call `svc.dispatch_view_transition_ready('firstPerson', success)` so the black overlay fades out into a scene that already has the polyline drawn.

Failure handling: if pathfinding fails, the helper still dispatches `viewTransitionReady` (so the user is not stranded on the black screen) and the inline error surfaces. **The user stays in first-person — we do not roll back to bird-eye on route failure.** The character is positioned at the FP entry point with the panel-level error available; rolling back the camera would be more disorienting than leaving them where they landed.

> **Anti-pattern (historic bug).** `NavigationOrchestratorService._ensure_auto_move` (in `younite.usd_viewer_player_core_extension/.../navigation_orchestrator_service.py`) flips the active route back to `"player"` on every ground click. It must clear **all** state fields the previous user route owns: `route_id`, `use_composer`, **and** `draw_path`. Forgetting `draw_path` causes the player point-click hops after a `poi_nav` / `seat_nav` / `quiet_zone_nav` route to inherit `drawPath=true` and render the sphere trail on every ground click — the regression first surfaced after the bird-eye → FP commit because that is the most common path that leaves the orchestrator with `draw_path=True`. Player point-click routes never want spheres; precalculated routes own their own `RouteInstance.draw_path` so their trails keep rendering for as long as that route is alive (intentional, "show paused route in background while exploring with point-click").

> **Anti-pattern (historic bug).** Earlier iterations used `teleport_to_coordinates` for POI / pin swaps — that only writes translate, leaving the bird-eye body rotation on the player and the camera's bird-eye pitch intact. The visible symptom was "facing completely wrong" after navigating from a restroom back to last-FP. The fix was the unified `start_pose` → `teleport_to_pose` path above; do not regress to translate-only teleports for view-transition flows.

> Anti-pattern: never gate the panel's mount/visibility on `routeReadyByRouteId` directly — the panel must stay visible during the calculating window. Gate the **stream overlay / FAB** layer instead, exactly like above.

### FP search widgets — `RestroomWidget` / `QuietZoneWidget` vs tracking screen

The CU **detail sheet** **“Get directions”** calls **`enterRestroomTrackingScreen` / `enterQuietZoneTrackingScreen`**, which sets **`restroomTrackingScreen` / `quietZoneTrackingScreen`** so the full-screen list unmounts (`return null` while tracking) and the **stream nav overlay** (play FAB, dismiss, measure) is meant to take over. That overlay is still gated on **`nav.routeReadyByRouteId['poi_nav'|'quiet_zone_nav'] === true`** (same `navmeshRouteReady` relay as above).

**Historic blank-screen bug:** tapping **Get directions** *before* Kit confirms the route hid the list while **`routeReady`** stayed false — **no FAB, no panel**. Mitigations wired in the web app:

- **Disable** the sheet **“Get directions”** button until the matching **`routeReadyByRouteId`** is true; expose **`title={t('search.poiWaitForRoute')}`** (`search.poiWaitForRoute` in all four locale files).
- On **`navmeshRouteError`** for **`poi_nav`** / **`quiet_zone_nav`**, **`navigationHandlers.ts`** clears **`restroomTrackingScreen` / `quietZoneTrackingScreen`** via setters passed through **`StreamOnlyWindow`** → **`EventStateSetters`** so the list can render again.
- Changing the active list selection or toggling the same item off resets tracking in **`usePoiNavigation`** / **`useQuietZoneNavigation`** so the user is not stuck off-widget after switching POIs.

## Future refactors / known duplication

These were noted during a review of the bird-eye seat directions feature; they are **pre-existing** code smells that grew slightly when the bird-eye seat directions flow was added. None are bugs — listed here so future work can consolidate them.

### 1. Waypoint planning is duplicated 4 ways

Same `waypoint_router` calls live in four spots with slightly different inputs:

| Where | Player ref | Section? | Reverse? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `extension.py` `_compute_poi_waypoints` (~line 156) | `/World/PlayerCharacter` prim | no (uses `plan_route_to_position`) | no |
| `extension.py` `_compute_bird_eye_waypoints` (~line 1103) | `/World/PlayerCharacter` prim | optional | no |
| `extension.py` `_on_seat_navigate` (~line 528, **inline**) | `/World/PlayerCharacter` prim | yes | no |
| `feature_commands_service/seat_directions.py` `SeatDirectionsHandler._run_directions` (**inline**) | `stored_fp_pos` (carb settings) | yes | yes (swap) |

The ideal consolidation would be a `waypoint_router.plan_seat_route(player_pos_or_path, seat_pos, section, reverse=False)` helper that internally resolves the `/World/PlayerCharacter` prim if a string is passed. That removes ~30 lines of inline boilerplate from each call site and makes the swap reversal a single parameter. Touches `extension.py` (pre-existing) so left for a dedicated refactor.

### 2. Seat arrival celebration logic duplicated across two handlers

`handleAutoMoveStatus` (auto-move arrival) and `handleRouteArrival` (proximity arrival) in `navigationHandlers.ts` contain near-identical seat_nav blocks: same `sendMessage` cleanup, same `revertWheelchairMode`, same swap-vs-normal celebration branch, same `schedulePoiArrival` call. This duplication **predates** the bird-eye seat directions feature — the swap branch I added inherited it.

A small `finalizeSeatArrival(s, { reason, fromSeat, manualNearDestination })` helper in `helpers.ts` would consolidate both blocks. Low risk because the two call sites already share the same structure.

### 3. Bird-eye seat highlight comments duplicate context

The block in `feature_commands_service/seat_directions.py:SeatDirectionsHandler._run_directions` and the matching note in `extension.py:_on_bird_eye_route_request` (where the highlight call was removed) both explain the same LOD-deactivation reasoning. If the seat-directions flow is ever consolidated into a single service, the explanation should live on `seat_highlight.py` (or its docstring).

